    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.diff\n\n### Description\n\nI really appreciate the versatility of `mini.diff` in computing diffs between arbitrary sources. To further enhance its strength, I propose adding more granular control over the diffs and sources.\r\n\r\n\r\n#### 1. Improved Word Diff\r\nIn the overlay visualization, word diffs are computed and shown, but there's no way to:\r\n\r\n* Move between individual word diffs within the same hunk/lines\r\n* Accept/reject word diffs individually\r\n\r\nInspired by [rickhowe/diffchar.vim](https://github.com/rickhowe/diffchar.vim), this feature would enhance user interaction with the diff output.\r\n\r\n#### 2. Line Range Diff Computation\r\nCurrently, `MiniDiff.set_ref_text` computes the diff between the whole buffer and the given text. I propose adding an option to restrict the diff computation to a specific line range.\r\n\r\nSimilar to [rickhowe/spotdiff.vim](https://github.com/rickhowe/spotdiff.vim), this feature would provide more fine-grained control over the diff process.\r\n\r\n### Rationale\r\nAlthough plugins like `diffchar.vim` and `spotdiff.vim` offer similar functionalities using Neovim's built-in diff tool, integrating them into another plugin is not straightforward. These plugins expose user commands but lack a comprehensive API. By incorporating these features into `mini.diff`, we can provide a more seamless experience for plugin authors.\r\n\r\n*Are these proposals aligned with the `mini.diff` spirit?*\r\n",
